so there you go um  lots of questions uh about uh the  allegations in parliament today it did  field in it the two uh interviews there  and no specific ideas for what should  change  going forward  well let's move on to some other  subjects now uh shall we vladimir  putin's war on ukraine is grinding on  relentlessly his forces continue to take  land in the donbass despite the brave  resistance there in response nato  leaders have approved a huge increase in  high readiness forces they're also going  to be deploying more troops to eastern  europe and that's led to a debate here  over whether it's time for us to spend  more on our defence and what we should  spend it on  well i'm pleased to say we're now joined  by the former chief of the defence staff  lord richard dannet thank you very much  for being with us this morning  sophie good morning i'm delighted to be  here  great well we're delighted to have you  um it felt like the beginning of the war  uh was really dominated by stories of  ukrainian success and russia failing to  meet their aims whether it was taking  keeve or  so on  but now is it right that that feels like  it's turning and that actually russia is  making steady grueling progress but  still progress in the east is russia now  winning the war  um  no i wouldn't say russia is winning the  war it all depends what  russia's objectives are considered to be  your analysis is absolutely right at the  start the ukrainians did extremely well  the russians were woefully inept as far  as their military is concerned and they  realized that their desired lightning  strike from belarus to kiev just was not  going to happen that was a shambles and  that episode that chapter finished and  then they started what we've been seeing  working out over the last few weeks and  months this relentless grind in the  donbass to take donetsk province and  luhans province and to join up with the  land corridor that the russians have  achieved already from crimea through  mario paul to to donbass but um the  russian tactics the russian way of doing  business has completely changed since  that early failed attack they're relying  on their huge superiority  in artillery pounding the provinces of  donetsk and luhansk  and then once they've almost reduced  everything to rubble then sending their  troops in to  to take possession of it and we're going  to see that slow grind continue  they very nearly achieved taking luhan's  province and then they will turn their  sights to trying to get the control of  donetsk now i think probably what we've  seen over the last few weeks shows that  over the next few days and weeks they  will probably achieve that and at that  stage i believe the russians will be  exhausted they won't have won the war  that they'll have achieved some of what  they want to achieve getting those two  don best provinces the ukrainians will  also be exhausted um so the russians  won't have one the ukrainians won't have  lost and at that point i believe that  the war will effectively go in the into  the deep freeze and that's where  meaningful negotiations are going to  have to start  well that's in a way is where the big  questions begin too  if as you say it's almost inevitable  that russia ends up taking those two  provinces in the donbass  what then should the international  community do  should we accept that russia holds on to  the territory it takes in return for  peace  well  it's interesting you put the question  that way we  shouldn't do anything in terms of  deciding what should happen um this has  got to be the ukrainians deciding what  is going to happen the worst outcome for  the west for those of us who have been  supporting ukraine is for us to apply  pressure on the ukrainians to come up  with some kind of compromise some kind  of deal with russia and for the  ukrainians then to criticize would be  justifiably criticized the west was  saying well your soldiers short what we  absolutely have to do in the west is  continue to support ukraine  unequivocally continue pouring in  weapons and ammunition which we're doing  and it's up to president zielinski and  the ukrainians to decide what  negotiations they want to have  with the russians  and the fact the matter is if one looks  at it objectively is that once the  russians have captured as i said yes  they will the two provinces have donets  and liberates completely and we've got  that land corridor the world might tell  them to go but the russians will not go  and nobody can make them go the russians  won't go voluntarily the ukrainians will  not be strong enough to throw them out  and of course the west nato has got no  intention whatsoever of mounting a  kuwait type sort of expulsion operation  such as we did  to get rid of sarah hussein's forces  over from kuwait that's just not going  to happen so the new reality is that the  sovereign territory of ukraine probably  is going to be occupied to about 20  by russian troops and that's the  accommodation that the ukrainians have  got to get their head around they must  get their head around it we mustn't make  them put their head around it  that's really interesting because in a  way i've kind of wrongly thought of this  as a decision moment for ukraine or for  you know the international community  whatever i think you either decide to  russia is going to keep this uh land or  you're going to try and repel russia but  actually  perhaps the most likely scenario is  russia  maintaining control of the donbass at  the war and deep freeze but just going  on  tragically and sadly that could be the  case now of course i don't know how far  you want to extend this conversation but  if we believe that's going to be the  scenario in ukraine  it then for the rest of the west and  particularly other countries that border  russia there is then the lingering  threat of a resurgent and domineering  russia  which is why nato at its summit quite  recently has said that it wants to  increase from forty thousand to three  hundred thousand high readiness troops  not to fight the russians although  troops must be trained and prepared to  fight the russians but to deter the  russians from further aggression uh  elsewhere whether it's in the baltic  states or poland or wherever  and again again extending the  conversation a bit more it comes back to  our domestic considerations  under some pressure the prime minister  has now agreed to increase our defense  budget to move towards 2.5 percent of  gdp by the end of the decade but  here's another quite narrow point but  important point we're still going ahead  in this country with reducing our army  by 10 000 troops over the next few years  frankly that's madness because if  nato is going to go to 300 000 high  readiness troops we've got to play our  part in that and reducing our army  further just makes no sense whatsoever  so domestically we welcome the rise to  2.5  of gdp but we must reverse the cuts in  numbers to the army and also increase  our investment in our army our land war  fighting capability i'm afraid the  future is not going to be returned to  things  pre-24th february the future is going to  be a grumpy resurgent russia on the  borders of countries in eastern europe  we've got to play our part in deterring  further russian aggression that's  interesting that you say that that cut  to troop number numbers is madness and  how what impact would it have  on our defense capability i mean i guess  in simple language are we going to be  less safe as a result  well we are in land force terms we are  much less capable than we were 10 years  ago um 10 15 years ago we were fighting  two difficult campaigns with our army in  iraq and afghanistan and doing them  simultaneously with an army of over a  hundred thousand we're coming down to an  army of seventy odd thousand and we  could not maintain operations at the  scale that we were doing 10 15 years ago  um  people say you shouldn't focus on the  number you should focus on capability  well in the case of our maritime and air  forces that is correct because those  services are platform based you've got  to have smart ships you've got to have  smart aircraft but when it comes to  old-fashioned fighting numbers do matter  and to have an army of only just over  seventy thousand  thirty percent down from where we were  ten years ago is going to leave us  woefully short if we find that we've got  to maintain large number of troops at  high readiness forward deployed in  eastern europe um it's a mere fact of  life we'll we'll break the remainder of  our army if our army is not large enough  are trained well enough and to quit well  enough to do what the government of the  day wants us to do you you can't make  people work endlessly um with two  smaller numbers so numbers in the terms  of our land forces really really matter  uh it's fascinating to listen to you um  now this week the former uh national  security adviser kim darick said that  nato has to be ready for a russian  nuclear strike  do you agree and what does that mean  well the west is always ready to respond  to a nuclear strike um but let's not get  carried away with this  we absolutely do not want to see  anybody using nuclear weapons in europe  or elsewhere  we all know from our history books what  the awful consequence was of the two  atom bombs uh in japan in 1945 modern  nuclear weapons are much more powerful  than that we don't even want to see a  small tactical nuclear weapon are being  used so um kim derek is perfectly  reasonable to say what he has said but  we should be under no illusion that our  british nuclear deterrent is always at  high readiness so is the united states  ready to watch whatever russia might or  might not do  i think the thing that's really  important here is to  keep this conflict in ukraine within the  concept of what we would call a limited  war limited in geography  and limited by the means that are being  used and one of the limits is the tacit  agreement that neither side will use  nuclear weapons they might say they're  prepared to do so what's really  important is that our defenses are ready  and i think the other thing people have  got to remember perhaps be a little bit  less fearful is vladimir putin can't get  up one morning and think well this is  not going very well i'm going to push  the button even within the russian  nuclear firing chain it requires a  number of people to confirm that  decision and confirm certain actions so  he can't just get out of bed in the  morning and push a button it's not as  simple as that but um important thing is  that our defenses are ready but let's  keep this below the nuclear threshold uh  and work it out in  i was going to say a civilized way well  what the russians are doing is not  civilized but in a at least a vaguely  rational way  okay i understood uh very briefly as we  are out of time but there is one more  question i do want to squeeze in  we've seen of course um these very  upsetting stories about british people  being accused of being mercenaries by  russia two more british men are captured  by the russians and again accused of  being mercenaries is there anything that  the government  can do in this situation  well i think they can make  representations to  russia through through the ukrainians  reminding that these are british  citizens but  but you know um if people for their own  reasons decide to get themselves  involved in somebody else's war they've  really got to take the consequences of  of their own actions now that said the  death sentence being passed on to former  british servicemen who have been held um  that's really quite outrageous those  those two for example were properly  enlisted in the ukrainian army and  therefore they should be treated like  prisoners of war be they ukrainian or be  they british um this whole business is  most unfortunate but i would certainly  say to anybody that's thinking of  going adventuring a little bit into  ukraine be very very careful  this actually is not your fight but if  you find yourself  being taken prisoner by the russians  then frankly um it may be out of your  control it may be even out of the  british government's hands  thank you so much for being on the  program this morning it's been really  interesting i certainly feel like i've  learned something from the interview  thank you

